scorer_views:
demand 5/2/2/3/2/2/2 pw125: At ~$0.08B, the 2026 base is far below the size floor; the niche qualifies only on its path to $1B, and the bear case (~20-25%, $0.25-0.9B) drops it from the universe. Demand-side signals are weak:
- the 800 V power rack is optional on Vera Rubin (~10% of VR racks expected in 2027);
- Kyber, the first 800 VDC-native platform, slipped to 2028;
- Diablo-400 is still at v0.7;
- Vertiv's validation may slip to 2027, with deployment in 2028;
- Sungrow's >130 MW of frameworks are non-binding against 1.2 GW of capacity;
- AC-fed 110 kW 54 V shelves are already shipping.
Conversion $/MW falls ~19%/yr, and no vendor discloses revenue. Probability-weighted 2030 is ~$2.15B, giving ~125% on the tiny base. Rank it on ~55% for 2027-30. Visibility, supply and evidence score 2; timing scores 3, with the inflection in 2027-28. || supply 5/2/2/3/2/2/2 pw110: I shade the base 2030 to ~$2.2B, because of the Kyber slip to 2028 and 800 V being optional on Vera Rubin, and weight in the bear (20-25% at ~$0.45B). That gives E[2030] of ~$1.8B, ~110-118% on an $0.08B base that fails the floor. The CAGR is mechanical: a one-quarter shift in Delta's revenue timing moves it 20-40 points, so rank on 2027-30 (~50% central, ~43% probability-weighted). Supply is loose. Sungrow's 1.2 GW of SST capacity alone is 2.7-17x global 2026 demand, Heron plans 40 GW, and Delta, Lite-On, Vertiv, Eaton, Schneider, Siemens, ABB and Chinese entrants all compete. The open Diablo-400 spec lets ODMs commoditize rectifiers, and conversion $/MW falls ~19%/yr. The binding constraints are standards (Diablo v0.7, UL/NEC above 600 VDC) and power ICs, not capacity, so supply is 2. 54 V AC shelves at 110 kW already ship and native-DC generation (Bloom) bypasses the conversion step, so substitution is 2. The main inflection is 2027-28 (timing 3). No vendor discloses 800 VDC revenue: sizes rest on broker percentages that mix in BBU and AC racks, and Sungrow's frameworks are non-binding, so visibility and evidence are both 2. || skeptic 5/2/2/3/2/2/2 pw110: Fails the 2026 size floor ($0.08B, range $0.04-0.18B). The 2030 path clears $1B in only ~75-80% of outcomes. The CAGR is mechanical off a near-zero base: a one-quarter shift in Delta's Q4-26 booking moves it 20-40 points, so rank on 2027-30 (~48% risk-weighted).

Skeptic flags:
- The status quo is shipping now: AC racks with 54 V 110 kW shelves. 800 VDC is optional on Vera Rubin.
- Kyber, the first 800 VDC-native platform, has slipped to 2028. Vertiv's deployment may slip to 2028, and Diablo is still at v0.7.
- Capacity is far from binding: Sungrow alone has 1.2 GW against 0.07-0.45 GW of 2026 global demand.
- ODMs can build to the open Diablo spec, so conversion-only $/MW falls ~19%/yr.
- Native-DC generation (Bloom) bypasses the conversion step, and SSTs collapse the LV rectifier stage.
- Being the discretionary layer, 800 VDC is deferred first in any 2028 capex cut.

Weighting gives ~$2.1B in 2030 (25% bear at ~$0.5B). No vendor discloses revenue, and broker percentages mix in BBU and AC racks.
